WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
<v Nora>Welcome to Long Horizon Notes. My guest today is Daniel Cho, former bond trader turned index advocate. Daniel, welcome back.</v>

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.240
<v Daniel Cho>Thanks, Nora. Glad to be here.</v>

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:17.700
<v Nora>Today's episode is supported by Betterment — automated investing that keeps your fees low and your plan boring. Visit betterment.com/podcast.</v>

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:24.000
<v Nora>I want to start with a warning that's aged well. Here's economist Nadia Sereni, from a talk back in 2016.</v>

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.580
<v Nadia Sereni>Leverage feels free until the day it isn't.</v>

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:32.880
<v Daniel Cho>That line held up. Most blow-ups I've seen weren't bad bets — they were fine bets financed the wrong way.</v>

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.420
<v Nora>So the risk isn't the asset, it's the borrowing against it.</v>

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:43.920
<v Daniel Cho>Right. An index fund you own outright is patient. The same fund on margin can be forced to sell at the worst possible moment.</v>

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.820
<v Nora>We'll leave the archive there. Daniel, thanks for coming back on.</v>

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.320
<v Daniel Cho>My pleasure, Nora.</v>

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.380
<v Nora>That's Long Horizon Notes for this week. Take care.</v>
